export const ALLERGY_POLICY_HTML = "<h2 class=\"jw-policy-h\">Document control and use</h2>\n<p>Owner: James Wallace, Director. James Wallace Education Ltd (registered in England and Wales, company number 17479328) is the operating entity. Content review is due 12 months after approval, or sooner after an incident, material service change, commissioner requirement or relevant law or guidance change.</p>\n<p>Scope: the agreed tutoring service in England, including home, approved community venues and online delivery. Staff means employees, workers and contractors when the procedure concerns their work; statutory employment entitlements depend on actual status. School-specific duties are applied only where the delivery role, law or commissioning terms make them applicable.</p>\n<p>Read with the Safeguarding and Welfare Framework (31), Operational Forms and Records (21), Safeguarding Contacts and Cover Annex (29) and Medical Needs and Medication (19). Before delivery the owner must approve the service scope, contacts, staff competence and arrangements relevant to the placement. Use an approved secure alternative during an outage; urgent help must never wait for a form or login.</p>\n<h2 class=\"jw-policy-h\">Scope and preparation</h2>\n<p>This procedure covers food and non-food allergies during home, community and online tutoring, including cafés, crafts, animals, insects and medicines. DfE's July 2026 allergy guidance is used as a safety benchmark; its direct statutory audience is specified schools and PRUs, so it is adopted here as best practice rather than as a duty that binds this service. The business does not hold spare adrenaline auto-injectors and does not rely on the school spare-AAI power; it relies on the learner's own prescribed device accompanying them. Consistent with 19 Medical Needs and Medication, the only medicine this service may give is the emergency use of the learner's own prescribed auto-injector to save life in anaphylaxis; no routine medicine is given.</p>\n<p>Before delivery ask about known/suspected allergies, triggers, previous reactions, prescribed medicines, clinical instructions and emergency contacts. Obtain an individual healthcare/allergy action plan proportionate to need, involving the learner, safe responsible adult and clinician where required. Record warning signs, exact prescribed response, device location, trained people, self-administration arrangements, handover and review. Do not diagnose an allergy or change a prescription. A missing diagnosis must not prevent emergency help.</p>\n<p>Check food labels each time, ingredients and cross-contact risk with the venue; consider shared utensils, cleaning, crafts and non-food materials. Do not describe a setting as guaranteed allergen-free. Avoid food sharing and unassessed food rewards. If safe controls cannot be established, select another activity/venue and involve the commissioner in the adjustment. Do not impose a blanket exclusion of learners with allergies. Treat allergy-related bullying as a safeguarding concern and follow 43 Anti-Bullying (Learners) and 30 Safeguarding and Child Protection.</p>\n<h2 class=\"jw-policy-h\">Devices and competence</h2>\n<p>The individual plan identifies prescribed rescue devices, quantity, expiry, storage and immediate access. Check that the agreed devices accompany the learner, including on community activities, and are not locked away from emergency access. Arrange replacements with the responsible adult before expiry; do not substitute another person's prescription informally. Device use is brand-specific and staff must know the instructions. If the agreed device is absent, expired or unusable on the day, the tutor does not proceed with an activity that carries the identified risk: the session is deferred or altered to remove the risk, and the responsible adult and, where the placement is commissioned, the commissioner are told before the session time. The fact, the decision and the notification are recorded on F02, F04 and F05.</p>\n<p><strong>Pre-deployment rule.</strong> No person may be allocated to deliver to a learner at risk of anaphylaxis until that person has (a) appropriate allergy-awareness training and (b) device- and learner-specific competence for that learner's prescribed rescue device and action plan, both recorded. Allergy awareness alone is not enough; device and learner-specific competence alone is not enough. The allocator, currently James Wallace as Director, checks and records both before the first session with that learner, and again after any change of device, plan or tutor.</p>\n<p>Three allergy-awareness courses completed by the owner are recorded in the training register (41 Supervision, Induction and Training) with provider and date: 'Allergy Awareness and Anaphylaxis' (Academize, 24 August 2026); 'Benedict's Law: Allergy and Anaphylaxis Safety' (My-Progression, 9 September 2026); 'Allergy Awareness 2026' (TP Tutors, 22 August 2026). None states a renewal date. A certificate alone does not evidence practical device assessment, competence for a particular learner or training for Asmaa or another tutor; those checks are recorded separately.</p>\n<p><strong>Current position at 26 September 2026.</strong> Allergy and anaphylaxis awareness is held by James Wallace (Academize, 24 August 2026; My-Progression, 9 September 2026; TP Tutors, 22 August 2026), which is the awareness training the emergency auto-injector exception requires; the business does not hold a spare auto-injector and relies on the learner's own prescribed device. Two things are still to be evidenced before the business delivers to a learner with a prescribed adrenaline auto-injector: recorded device- and learner-specific competence for that learner's device and action plan, and the practical first-aid position for that learner. Emergency First Aid at Work is booked for 12 October 2026 and is not held before then; paediatric first aid is not required by default and is reconsidered for each placement where the specification or the learner's needs require it (17 Health and Safety). Until a named person with recorded device competence for that learner is present for the session, the business accepts no placement for a learner with a prescribed adrenaline auto-injector, and tells the commissioner and family before the session time. For home and community delivery that person must be physically present. For online delivery the tutor cannot handle the device, so a named competent adult must be physically present with the learner and confirmed before the session, recorded on F02 and F05; without that confirmation no online session is delivered to a learner with a prescribed adrenaline auto-injector. The named competent person for each placement is recorded in the placement record, their competence evidence on F03 (competence records), and the session cover on F05 (first aid and session cover).</p>\n<h2 class=\"jw-policy-h\">Suspected anaphylaxis: act immediately</h2>\n<p>Recognise sudden airway/breathing difficulty, throat/tongue swelling, collapse or marked altered responsiveness; a rash may be absent. Use the available prescribed adrenaline auto-injector promptly following its instructions and the learner's plan, and call 999 for an ambulance, stating suspected anaphylaxis. This is the limited statutory exception to the prescription-only restriction for a medicine specified in Schedule 19 used to save life in an emergency (Human Medicines Regulations 2012, reg 238 and Schedule 19, which lists \"Adrenaline 1:1000 up to 1mg for intramuscular use in anaphylaxis\"). If alone, use speakerphone where possible. Do not wait for a parent, manager or portal entry.</p>\n<p>Keep the person lying down; if breathing is difficult, support the position advised by the emergency dispatcher. Do not let them stand or walk, even if improving. If symptoms have not improved after five minutes, use a second available prescribed auto-injector following instructions. Follow 999 advice, including CPR if required, and arrange ambulance access. Antihistamine must not delay adrenaline or emergency help. Continue the emergency response even if the person improves.</p>\n<p>For an online session, stay on the call, confirm the learner's exact address and a contactable adult, call 999, and follow the dispatcher's advice; a remote tutor cannot reach the device but must direct the response and summon help. For online delivery to a learner with a prescribed adrenaline auto-injector, the competent adult required by the current-position rule above must be with the learner and confirmed before the session; the tutor does not begin the session without that confirmation.</p>\n<h2 class=\"jw-policy-h\">Aftercare and records</h2>\n<p>Give responders the clinical plan, device/dose times and known trigger; notify the safe responsible adult, the duty safeguarding lead (DSL Asmaa Ahmed; deputy DSL James Wallace, reachable on 07897 021077 or hello@jameswallace.tech, with the duty rota, further numbers and the out-of-hours fallback in the Safeguarding Contacts and Cover Annex (29)) and the commissioning authority or referrer the same working day, and immediately if the learner is taken to hospital or the incident is serious. If neither lead can be reached, the matter is escalated without waiting through the urgent routes in that annex, the local children's social care front door, the out-of-hours service, or 999 where there is immediate danger. Record the incident/near miss, exposure, symptoms, medicines, calls, times, handover and learning on F04 and pass the clinical facts to responders. Replace used devices and review the plan, venue and staff competence before the next relevant session. Forms F02, F03 and F04 support these records. This procedure is rehearsed with both named leads before the first placement and at least annually after that; the date, participants and any learning are recorded in the training register (41). Clinical questions about a specific learner's plan are referred to the prescriber or specialist nurse named in that plan. Allergy, healthcare and incident records are kept and disposed of under the Records Retention Schedule (46): health, medical and allergy records are kept until the learner's 25th birthday; the shorter 3-year-after-leaving period applies only to routine records with no safeguarding, medical-claim or commissioner relevance, so it is not used for allergy records (46 Records Retention Schedule; 19 Medical Needs and Medication). Incident records are kept as that schedule provides.</p>\n<h2 class=\"jw-policy-h\">Sources and change record</h2>\n<p>Version 2.0 (26 September 2026). New tailored procedure; records certificate evidence without overstating practical competence or commissioner acceptance. Sources are registered in 00 Statutory and Guidance Register (entries G6 and G7; the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 and the Health and Safety (First-Aid) Regulations 1981 are registered at S4 and S6).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>allergy: <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/allergy-safety-in-schools\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/allergy-safety-in-schools</a></li>\n<li>medical: <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/supporting-pupils-at-school-with-medical-conditions--3\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/supporting-pupils-at-school-with-medical-conditions--3</a></li>\n<li>anaphylaxis: <a href=\"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/anaphylaxis/\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/anaphylaxis/</a></li>\n<li>firstaid: Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 s.3, 'General duties of employers and self-employed to persons other than their employees' (<a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1974/37/section/3\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1974/37/section/3</a>); HSE first-aid guidance for employers: <a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/firstaid/what-employers-need-to-do.htm\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.hse.gov.uk/firstaid/what-employers-need-to-do.htm</a> (about employees; applied here by analogy to learners and others affected by the work)</li>\n<li>emergency adrenaline: Human Medicines Regulations 2012, reg 238 (administration of certain medicines in an emergency) and Schedule 19: <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2012/1916/regulation/238\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2012/1916/regulation/238</a> ; <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2012/1916/schedule/19\" rel=\"noopener\">https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2012/1916/schedule/19</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The owner records adoption, briefings, applicable commissioner clauses and implementation evidence in F10, the master register (21). Preserve the prior version; do not backdate approval.</p>";
